I was finally able to track the problem down myself:

the misbehaviour of the DS10 was caused by ipf due to strange
rulesets.
Outgoing traffic made use of the keep state rule, while the inbound
open ports did not have that flag set.
e.g.:

pass in quick from any to any port=22
pass out quick from xx.xx.xx.xx to any keep state
block in quick on tu0 from any to any
block in quick on tu1 from any to any

changing that to:

pass in quick from any to any port=22 keep state
pass out quick from xx.xx.xx.xx to any keep state
block in quick on tu0 from any to any
block in quick on tu1 from any to any

somehow resolved the problem. Afaik this is the correct way to do it
in the first place - but I have no idea, why this discriminated
between windows / debian sarge clients on one side and ubuntu clients
on the other side?

Again many thanks to everybody,
Regards,
Bjoern


