Hi Artem,

I try to remember the needed tasks, because I cannot test it right now.

On V4.0* the problem needs some more work, before you get it corrected.

First you have to boot the generic kernel from the OS CD and mount the 
system disk root partition and the /usr partition to temporary mount 
points. Then you have to run sizer (-n TEMP) then compare the /tmp/TEMP 
file and the /sys/conf/YOURSYSTEM to find the new scsi controller entry 
in the /tmp/TEMP file. Then you have to add that line into the 
/sys/conf/YOURSYSTEM file and rebuild the kernel and copy it to the root 
partition and then boot to single user from the system disk. Now you can 
try to run the bcheckrc script. If the disk was found and the needed 
device files were generated the other filesystems will be mounted. If 
not, you have to cd to the /dev directory and run the MAKEDEV rz* <--- 
whichever the disks (new) scsi address is. You have to edit the 
/etc/fstab to correct the new disk name for the mounts. But if you are 
using Advfs, you have to correct the links in the /etc/fdmns/*_domain 
directories to the new disk device name.
Then you should be able to run the bcheckrc and then boot up to run level 3.


On 5.1* many tasks are easier and don't need so much work.
This problem should be solved by booting the generic kernel to single 
user, run hwmgr -scan scsi (which should find all the needed devices and 
their possibly new addresses. After that you should be able to run the 
bcheckrc to get the other filesystems mounted and boot up to run level 3.

Hope I didn't forget something critical.

Good Luck!

Regards,

Kari


