In 4.0g I solved the problem as follows.
The 4.0g system was able to boot with the generic kernel to the 
multui-user mode, which I found wierd because the /usr should not have 
been found. Then I edited the kernel config file to mention to correct 
PCI slots for all PCI devices (Like "controller isp0 at pci0 slot 2")
And than it was solved.

However, 5.1 has no PCI controller mappings in the kernel config files. 
Therefore the trick does not work there. The problem is that the system 
rememers that there was a SCSI controller isp0 in slot 2 and now once it 
is in in slot 3 it calls in isp1. So if I could just delete that 
controller entry for the former isp0, I believe everything would work 
fine without renaming the entries in fstab and other places. The problem 
is that I do not want to edit fstab and other things, because I do not 
know what the application software does with the disk names.

It would be cleaner to remap the isp0 to a different slot than remapping 
everything else from isp0 to isp1. We have to do that at several 
customers, so it needs to be a minimal change.

In 5.1. I was able to reach some result by hwmgr and dsfmgr to remove 
old dsk0 and rename dsk1 to dsk0.


Regards,
Artem

