Hi Artem,

Good to hear that you got the V4.0 part done.

But with V5.1* it should be even easier and you don't have to hack the 
kernel configuration, because the device mapping is dynamic there. When 
you boot the kernel it walks through the hardware and maps all found 
(supported) hardware to appropriate device files. There is a clever 
feature in V5.*, which helps a lot when you are making changes in your 
disk configuration. It keeps record about all SCSI-devices (and 
FC-devices) by their WWID, which is created at the factory for newer 
devices. For older devices Tru64 creates an unique "WWID" which consists 
of some scsi information from the device. The WWID and the device file 
is then mapped together in the device database.
Therefore, if you have had a disk in your Tru64 V5.* machine at least 
once, it keeps that recorded until you delete it (manually) and whenever 
you bring that disk back into the machine and place it somewhere ( on 
any SCSI bus) the OS will bring it up with the original device file mapping.
So in this case you shouldn't need to do anything else than to make the 
OS find the disk and then it is ready to use like it was before. Thanks 
to this feature you don't have to keep updating the kernel 
configuration, the /etc/fstab or the /etc/fdmns records and not fiddle 
with the device files.

One mapping which you must always update - if the disk is the boot disk 
- is the boot device (bootdef_dev) in SRM console. E.g. if the disk was 
originally DKA100 and after moving it it is DKB300, you have to update 
that information.


Regards,

Kari







